Q: How to create an image of a font&#39;s characters? A: <p>I know you explicitely asked for a solution that allowed you to export individual pictures of each single character of a specific characters set you decided upfront.</p>
<p>But you also said:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Without installing these fonts on my own system, I would like to create an HTML gallery of these fonts.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Well if your constraint is to avoid installing fonts on a given system and just have the chance to view how some chosen characters shows up when rendered with a font from a file you have stored locally in any of these formats: <code>ttf</code>, <code>otf</code>, <code>woff</code>, <code>woff2</code></p>
<p>...you can achieve that exact result by using a <code>@font-face</code> rule that won't require to install any font. The font file will be referenced as a filesystem relative path, allowing you to open that web page in any system with no prior installation as long as you have a web browser available.</p>
<p><a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/Reference/At-rules/@font-face" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/Reference/At-rules/@font-face</a></p>
<p>Of course this won't fulfill your very specific request if you really needed a picture for each single grlyph, but I wanted to attempt a proposed solution since this one still fulfill the conditions you stated so far on why you preferred pictures over font files.</p>
<p>This is a very simple demo showing some predetermined glyphs from a secific font file.</p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="true" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-js lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>function changeFontSize(value){
  document.querySelector('.specimen')
    .style.fontSize = value + 'px'
}

function populateSizes(sizeSelect, sizes, selected){

  sizes.map(size =&gt; {
    const sizeOption = document.createElement('option');
    sizeOption.value = size;
    sizeOption.textContent = `${size}px`;
    if(size == selected) sizeOption.selected = true;
    sizeSelect.append(sizeOption);
  });
}

document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', ()=&gt;{
  const sizes = [
    12, 14, 16, 18, 20, 24, 28, 32, 40, 48, 64, 80, 96
  ];
  const sizeSelect = document.getElementById('sizeSelect');
  populateSizes(sizeSelect, sizes, 28);
  changeFontSize(24);
});</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-css lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>@font-face {
  font-family: 'MyFont';
  /* this could be also url('./fonts/alpine.woff') */
  src: url('https://fonts.cdnfonts.com/s/53201/Alpine.woff');
}

.specimen {
  font-family: 'MyFont';
}

#sizeSelect{
  cursor: pointer;
}</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-html lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;div class="specimen"&gt;
  ABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZ&lt;br&gt;
  abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz&lt;br&gt;
  0123456789!@#$%^&amp;*()
&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;select id="sizeSelect" onchange="changeFontSize(this.value)"&gt;
&lt;/select&gt;</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>

Q: How to adjust CSS via media query to a dynamically set orientation during printing? A: <p>As <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/a/79896467/32233055">Diegos detailed answer</a> answer points out, this shouldn't be possible as it goes against spec.</p>
<p>Fun Facts:</p>
<ul>
<li><p>Edge goes against spec so it works and you can actually get it to run an endless loop by doing this:</p>
<pre class="lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>
@page {
  size: a4 landscape;
}

@media print and (orientation: landscape) {
  @page {
    size: a4 portrait;
  }
}
</code></pre>
</li>
<li><p>Firefox also goes against spec and updates the media query environment once when you change a setting like the number of printed pages or color mode.</p>
</li>
</ul>
